{"kind":"assert","text":"SECURITY AUDIT — CORRECTION AND EXTENSION (addressed to builder, from checker). This supersedes the closing assessment of my earlier security assert 3a681aec. A second, deeper review pass found that the JSONata confinement has MORE than the one hole I reported there. My earlier assert said \"the confinement design is strong; S1 is the one real hole in it\" — that was WRONG and overconfident. There are at least three distinct confinement escapes in the admitted JSONata subset, and they share one root cause: the subset is enforced by regex/substring lexing of the source TEXT, which cannot see operator-driven constructs (the ~\u003e apply operator, the λ lambda synonym, or \u0026 string growth). The durable fix is to validate the compiled AST from jsonata.Compile — reject any node of type lambda/partial/transform/apply, and any variable/function node whose name is outside the allowlist — instead of pattern-matching source text. I verified the two new confinement escapes below with reproduction tests at head 1a26954, and the third (H1) by code inspection.\n\nH-NEW-1 (HIGH, VERIFIED by reproduction) — the ~\u003e apply operator reaches the ENTIRE unary-builtin set, defeating both the function allowlist and the object-wildcard exclusion. jsonataCallRE only tests functions written as $name(...); the apply operator invokes a function by bare reference with no parentheses (evaluateApplyExpression in the pinned v206). I compiled folds containing `event.record ~\u003e $keys`, `~\u003e $spread`, `~\u003e $reverse`, `~\u003e $sort`, and `~\u003e $base64encode` — ALL compiled cleanly, none are in the 20-name allowlist. Critically, `$keys` and `$spread` enumerate an object's keys and key/value pairs — exactly the \"observable object wildcards\" that hasUnquotedAsterisk was written to forbid (its own comment says so). So a hostile fold reads and exfiltrates arbitrary telemetry structure via `event.record ~\u003e $spread` despite the wildcard ban. (The one saving grace: ambientJSONataRE matches the bare $now/$millis/$random/$shuffle/$eval names without requiring a paren, so those ambient/nondeterministic functions do NOT escape via ~\u003e.) Fix: reject the ~\u003e token and any $name reference not in the allowlist in the interim; validate the AST for the durable fix.\n\nH-NEW-2 (HIGH, VERIFIED by reproduction) — the λ codepoint is a full synonym for `function`, bypassing the lambda ban. jsonataLambdaRE = `\\bfunction\\s*\\(` catches only the ASCII spelling; the pinned parser treats λ identically. Defining `λ($s){ ... }` is not caught. Alone this is limited because CALLING the lambda via `$name(` hits the allowlist — but combined with H-NEW-1 it is a clean escape: I compiled AND cleanly evaluated `( $double := λ($s){ $s \u0026 $s }; $r := \"x\" ~\u003e $double ~\u003e $double; ... )` — a λ-defined lambda applied via ~\u003e with no $name( anywhere, so neither the lambda regex nor the allowlist fires. This gives arbitrary user-defined functions and, via self-application through ~\u003e, recursion (bounded only by MaxDepth=64 and the 250ms wall clock). Fix: reject `\\b(?:function|λ)\\s*\\(` in the interim; AST validation (reject lambda/apply nodes) for the durable fix.\n\nH-NEW-3 (HIGH, VERIFIED by inspection) — OTLP record fan-out is fully materialized BEFORE the record-count limit is checked, and the per-group resource map is re-serialized per record. In internal/ingest/receiver.go the switch does `records, err = flattenLogs/Traces/Metrics(...)` (lines 121/129/137), and only afterward `if len(records) \u003e receiver.limits.Records` (line 150). flatten builds every record, and makeRecord (canonical.go:200) does json.Marshal(canonical) where canonical embeds the whole per-group `resource` map (canonical.go:55/89/140). Exploit: readBody admits 16MiB decompressed / 4MiB gzip; one ResourceLogs with a ~200KiB resource-attributes map plus 10,000 empty ~2-byte LogRecords re-serializes that 200KiB resource 10,000 times → ~2GB retained from a sub-kilobyte gzip payload, all before the count limit is consulted; only inbox.Enqueue's byte cap then rejects it, post-allocation. The receiver semaphore permits 16 concurrent such requests (~32GB). This is a loopback-reachable whole-engine OOM by any same-user subprocess (the population the threat model contemplates). Fix: thread a running count+byte budget into the flatten functions and abort as soon as count \u003e limits.Records or accumulated canonical bytes exceed a ceiling (\u003c= the inbox byte limit); pre-check the resource/scope canonical size once per group since it is multiplied by record count.\n\n(H4/S1 from assert 3a681aec — the \u0026 concatenation + := binding geometric-allocation OOM — stands; I reproduced 12GB from a 1498-byte program. H-NEW-1/2 and S1 all point at the same durable fix: bound the evaluator's allocation/steps and validate the AST rather than the source text.)\n\nADDITIONAL FINDINGS from the deeper pass (M4/M5 verified by me; M1/M3 mechanism confirmed against the code):\nM-A (Medium) — one engine mutex serializes queries, folds, and ingestion, contradicting the architecture note's stated invariant that \"a cancelled or over-budget SQL query does not stop ingestion or folding.\" Engine.Query holds e.mu across query.Open (file opens + up to 8 ATTACHes) and the full 5s-bounded sandbox.Query, and Engine.accept (the ingest path) takes the same e.mu; Activate's drainLocked runs with waveLimit 0 (drain-to-empty) under e.mu. So an over-budget query does stall ingestion, and a long drain stalls status/query/shutdown. Fix: snapshot paths/profiles/frontiers under e.mu, release it, then run query.Open/Query outside the lock (projections are already read-only per request); cap and deadline the activation drain.\nM-B (Medium) — declared reads are fully materialized before MaxInputBytes is enforced. projection.executeRead scans all rows into memory (a MANY LIMIT 1024 read over a JSON column can hold ~256MiB) and evaluationInput assembles them before Evaluate does json.Marshal(input) and only then checks len \u003e MaxInputBytes. Fix: accumulate a byte budget inside the rows.Next() loop and abort early; also set SQLite LIMIT_LENGTH on the projection connection (it is set on the query connection but not in projection.openDatabase).\nM-C (Medium, VERIFIED) — CREATE INDEX skips validateQueryShape entirely. compiler.go's createIndexRE is unanchored at the end and the case checks only ambientSQLRE before appending the statement verbatim to ReplaceableSQL; the \"no SQL functions\", SELECT *, and qualified-relation rules never apply, there is no nameExists collision check, and unique indexes created here are invisible to Table.UniqueKeys/StorageShape and thus to ContinueCompatible. I traced it as CONTAINED not exploitable today (scratch DB holds only app relations; TRUSTED_SCHEMA=off + DEFENSIVE reject non-innocuous functions), but it is the weakest DDL path and the one most likely to become exploitable under a change. Fix: anchor the regex, parse ON table(expr-list)[WHERE ...], require a declared target table, run validateQueryShape over the expression/predicate, and add the index name to nameExists.\nM-D (Medium, VERIFIED — privacy) — deleting a tailapp leaves its full materialized projection on disk. Engine.Delete detaches obligations, closes the projection, and deletes registry rows, but never removes projections/\u003cname\u003e/state.sqlite (+ -wal/-shm), and definition_revisions rows are never deleted (no FK cascade). Telemetry-derived tables can contain prompts, commands, paths, and identifiers, so this defeats the user-visible meaning of \"delete\" that docs/authoring.md and docs/reference/mcp.md describe. Fix: os.RemoveAll the projection directory in Delete (or add an explicit purge), cascade definition_revisions, and document the choice.\nLow-severity items also worth a look: mounted-schema table/column names can be enumerated via SQLite's distinct \"no such table\" vs \"access prohibited\" error text (query.go passes both through verbatim) — normalize to one opaque relation_not_available; WAL/SHM sidecars and the control socket are created before their chmod (mitigated by $TAILAPP_HOME 0700, defense-in-depth only) — set umask 0077 around creation; telemetry fragments can reach gap_reason and control error bodies — classify to stable codes and drop free-form payload text (this is the same concern as S4 in 3a681aec); an unbounded mounts map in one query request does ~one Frontier round-trip per alias under e.mu before LIMIT_ATTACHED=8 is enforced — reject len(mounts) \u003e 8 up front; splitStatements does not track [..] or backtick quoting (can only over-split into a rejection, not smuggle); compileProgram locates USING with a plain substring search rather than the \\bUSING\\b regex (fragile, not an escape).\n\nCHECKED AND SOUND (re-confirmed by the deeper pass, no action): projection identifier quoting; the default-deny authorizer including AUTH_PRAGMA and the quoted-relation mount bypass (FROM \"b\".\"private\" is caught); query_only + the full Limit() set; ATTACH-before-SetAuthorizer with '-doubling and mode=ro; validateSourcePath and the Lstat symlink checks on all three DB open paths; the gzip compressed-before/decompressed-LimitReader bounds; inbox capacity checked before insert; validateOutput's DisallowUnknownFields + trailing-value rejection; ambientJSONataRE catching bare $now/$millis/$eval (so ambient functions do NOT escape via ~\u003e); export/view compilation against a scratch DB lacking the platform tables. The confinement is well-built at the SQL layer; the JSONata layer is where the source-text-lexing approach leaks, and moving that layer to AST validation closes H-NEW-1, H-NEW-2, and S1 together."}